# Firmware (ESP32 motor control)

This is the code that actually drives the rover's motors. It runs on the ESP32 and just
sits there listening on serial for commands like `L:150,R:-100`, then spins the motors
accordingly. Eventually the line-detection code will be the one sending these commands,
but for now you can just type them into the Serial Monitor to test.

## Wiring

Using a basic dual H-bridge driver (L298N-style, two enable pins + 4 direction pins):

```
ENA -> 25   (left speed / PWM)
IN1 -> 26   (left direction)
IN2 -> 27   (left direction)

ENB -> 33   (right speed / PWM)
IN3 -> 32   (right direction)
IN4 -> 18   (right direction)
```

## How to flash it

Using PlatformIO:

```bash
cd firmware
pio run -t upload
pio device monitor -b 115200
```

## Command format

Send this over serial, one line at a time:

```
L:<num>,R:<num>
```

Numbers can be -255 to 255. Positive = forward, negative = reverse, 0 = stop.

Example — spin in place:
```
L:150,R:-150
```

If it doesn't understand what you sent, it'll tell you:
- Wrong format (no `L:`/`,R:` found) → `Invalid Format. Use L:<val>,R:<val>`
- Not actually a number (like `L:abc,R:100`) → `Invalid Value: Provide integer values`

## What's built in so far

**If serial goes quiet, it stops on its own.**
If no valid command shows up for 200ms, both motors ramp down to zero automatically.
This is basically so it doesn't keep driving forever if the laptop/line-detection board
crashes or the cable comes loose — figured that's more important to get right before
anything else since this thing can drive off on its own.

**Motors ramp instead of jumping.**
If you go from full forward to full reverse, it doesn't slam the direction instantly —
it steps toward the new target a little bit each loop. Keeps the current draw sane and
stops the rover from jerking around, which matters more once line-detection is sending
rapidly changing values.

**PWM is at 5kHz.**
Started at 100Hz which made the motors audibly whine. 5kHz is smooth and quiet.

**There's a PWM floor.**
Anything above 0 gets mapped so it's never below `MIN_PWM` (10), so it doesn't try to
move at a PWM so low the motor just sits there buzzing without actually turning.

## Constants you might want to tweak later

These are near the top of `main.cpp`:

- `COMMAND_TIMEOUT` (currently 200ms) — how long before it decides comms are dead and stops
- `RAMP_STEP` (currently 5) — how fast it ramps to a new speed. Lower = smoother but slower to react
- `MIN_PWM` (currently 10) — minimum PWM before it stops bothering to try

Haven't actually road-tested these numbers on real hardware yet, just went with reasonable
starting guesses. Will probably need adjusting once I see how it actually drives.

## Stuff I still need to add

- A hard cap on max speed (don't want this thing going full send by accident)
- Some kind of dedicated STOP command, separate from the L:/R: format, just in case
- Might eventually swap off Arduino's `String` class for parsing if it ever seems to
  cause memory issues running for a long time — not a problem yet, just a "someday" thing